  "query_preview": "..\nToday: The ridge continues to bring a stable conditions across \nthe region. Residual moisture in the boundary layer is bringing \nan fog and low ceilings for valleys and portions of the Basin \nthrough the morning. Highs for the period will be in the 50s to \nlow 60s. Lows will be in the upper 30s and 40s. \n\nFriday through Tuesday: With the ridge axis shifting East late \nThursday, a series of Lows will push through the region. The \ntrack of the Lows are south of the Pacific Northwest. The tracks\nkeep the Inland Northwest out of the higher moisture and \nvorticity areas around the Lows. It leads to weaker shower \nactivity across the region. Precipitable water values struggle \nto get above an inch with the systems. Lowland areas have a \n25-75 probability of 0.2-0.5 of an inch Friday through Tuesday. \nMountains have a higher spread of 0.5-1 inch. The period will \ntrend cooler with snow levels dropping to around 5000 ft by \nSaturday morning. Cascade Passes can expect snow. Stevens having\na 25-75 percent probability of 0 to 2 inches and Washington \nPass with a possible 3 to 6 inches from Friday into Tuesday. \nBasin winds are breezy during Friday and Saturday afternoons \nwith gusts into the 20-25 mph range. Highs for the period will \nbe in the 40s to low 50s. Lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s.\n/JDC",
